(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{y}$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $f$ exist." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381048 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb51f4b0-9af5-582f-b38f-08847728e4c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381048 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{f}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${f}_{x}(x,y)=-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{y}$ is defined only when $y\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${f}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{x}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$\n\n- Similarly, the domain of ${f}_{y}$ is the same: \n\n$$\n{D}_{{f}_{y}}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $f$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}\\cap \\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\} \\\\  & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\ne 0\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381049 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186540929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381049 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381049 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381049 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186540886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381049 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d1f6e4dc-6584-5b6a-8780-9caeb5fe7ea6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186445248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382208 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 175921864452